AUDIO: 'in the US. Flock claims it’s a public safety&nbsp; technology provider, giving communities the&nbsp;&nbsp; tools they need to solve crime while putting them&nbsp; in control of their data. Its critics say they’re&nbsp;&nbsp; a venture capital funded surveillance system that&nbsp; has trouble securing data and preventing abuse.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Flock aren’t the only ALPRs, or automated license&nbsp; plate readers, or even the only ones that allow&nbsp;&nbsp; searches across its national database. What is&nbsp; different about Flock is the speed at which it has&nbsp;&nbsp; grown, the size it has reached, and the new places&nbsp; it has pushed surveillance. The company claims it&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reads 20 billion license plates a month for more&nbsp; than 5,000 communities and over 4,800 police&nbsp;&nbsp; departments. Even when Flock is used as designed&nbsp; by the police in the investigation of a crime,&nbsp;&nbsp; it can flag the wrong person, forcing&nbsp; someone who did nothing wrong to prove&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:04] [scene:talk]
